7ed85","name":"stitch-S-5-normal-79ac331a.png","screen":"S-5","screenId":"e0d7de4ec17d42bda1796439d1eb7bba","state":"normal"},"ST-8":{"html":"3390640f3294eaadd90f7b2da5acaee745f486615bf8e3a319b1c550c232db36","image":"c63eab73ada3c107f795a37870e4e6e39a85d1648b2591a42b1ebaa04838aa25","name":"stitch-S-5-empty-c63eab73.png","screen":"S-5","screenId":"33365e970a394099a57104f6de28e689","state":"empty"},"ST-9":{"html":"c96d09b725a69847981cdef83914c7eb50f3ab4a069a02bb534a3cb2bc946fe6","image":"314accf7bfa156605ff8d8d1c63634a974a8cc5d5c8f2922ea69e9cc2d972e6f","name":"stitch-S-6-normal-314accf7.png","screen":"S-6","screenId":"f3f8ee52b92f46589f87fb0027a769ab","state":"normal"}},"links":{"S-1":[{"label":"Continue with Google","to":"S-2"}],"S-10":[{"label":"Overview","to":"S-6"},{"label":"Employees","to":"S-7"},{"label":"Parking Settings","to":"S-8"},{"label":"Access & Reports","to":"S-9"}],"S-2":[{"label":"Available Spaces","to":"S-3"},{"label":"Release Schedule","to":"S-4"},{"label":"My Vehicles","to":"S-5"}],"S-3":[{"label":"My Parking","to":"S-2"},{"label":"Release Schedule","to":"S-4"},{"label":"My Vehicles","to":"S-5"}],"S-4":[{"label":"My Parking","to":"S-2"},{"label":"Available Spaces","to":"S-3"},{"label":"My Vehicles","to":"S-5"}],"S-5":[{"label":"My Parking","to":"S-2"},{"label":"Available Spaces","to":"S-3"},{"label":"Release Schedule","to":"S-4"}],"S-6":[{"label":"Employees","to":"S-7"},{"label":"Parking Settings","to":"S-8"},{"label":"Access & Reports","to":"S-9"},{"label":"Utilization","to":"S-10"}],"S-7":[{"label":"Overview","to":"S-6"},{"label":"Parking Settings","to":"S-8"},{"label":"Access & Reports","to":"S-9"},{"label":"Utilization","to":"S-10"}],"S-8":[{"label":"Overview","to":"S-6"},{"label":"Employees","to":"S-7"},{"label":"Access & Reports","to":"S-9"},{"label":"Utilization","to":"S-10"}],"S-9":[{"label":"Overview","to":"S-6"},{"label":"Employees","to":"S-7"},{"label":"Parking Settings","to":"S-8"},{"label":"Utilization","to":"S-10"}]},"model":"stitch-default","motion":{"ambient":"Only a genuinely live availability status dot breathes from 0.94 to 1 opacity on a 7.5s `ease-in-out infinite` cycle; no other perpetual loop runs.","signature":"Bay-mark handoff: a parking record’s title and thin green row rule carry from worklist to detail via `view-transition-name: parking-record` over 420ms with `cubic-bezier(0.65, 0, 0.35, 1)`."},"projectId":"16352890729195438428","prompts":{"S-1":"Design the Folio3 Parking sign-in page as a finished responsive web page. The first thing the eye meets is the Folio3 Parking wordmark and a clear, medium-large heading, “Parking for Folio3 employees”; keep the page quiet and uncluttered rather than using a hero banner. Place one prominent touch-sized “Continue with Google” button beneath a short note that access is limited to active Folio3 employees using their corporate Google account. Add a small privacy/security note explaining that the account is checked before access is granted. Keep generous intentional empty space around the sign-in panel. Use the product design system’s assigned palette and typography; no decorative illustrations or stock people. This page is responsive at phone, tablet, and desktop widths.","S-10":"Design the Parking Administrator’s Utilization page with the shared administrator navigation in the exact order “Overview”, “Employees”, “Parking Settings”, “Access & Reports”, “Utilization”; mark “Utilization” active. Lead with a compact report heading and reporting period: “Weekly utilization · May 11–17, 2026” and “Generated Mon, May 18, 2026 · 8:00 AM · Folio3 office time”. Directly below, show concise, tabular summary figures: 246 used allocations / 300 eligible allocations · 82%; 54 unused allocations, split into 42 dedicated and 12 temporary; 21 released spaces; 19 accepted claims. Include daily utilization rows for May 11–15: 50/60 · 83%, 52/60 · 87%, 49/60 · 82%, 51/60 · 85%, and 44/60 · 73%; show the week’s calculation as 100 × used allocations ÷ eligible allocations, or 0% if there are none. Below the summary, provide compact filters for date range, floor, allocation type, and used/unused status, a touch-sized “Apply filters” action, and “Export CSV”. Follow with a filterable records table with realistic employee-identifying examples: Maya Chen · Dedicated · May 11 · P2 · Vehicle detected · Used; Arjun Patel · Temporary · May 11 · P1 · No vehicle detected · Unused; Priya Nair · Dedicated · May 12 · P3 · Vehicle detected · Used. Where people or vehicles are shown visually, use real photographs of the named employees and registered vehicles, never illustrations, icon substitutes, or grey boxes. State plainly that this is monitoring and reporting only: no automated warnings, suspensions, or penalties. Keep the table compact, use status lozenges, put the filters beside the weekly summary on wide screens, and stack on phones; preserve touch-sized controls and responsive behavior across phone, tablet, and desktop.","S-2":"Design the employee’s My Parking page in Folio3 Parking. Use the shared employee navigation in this exact order: “My Parking”, “Available Spaces”, “Release Schedule”, “My Vehicles”. Make “My Parking” active. The first visual anchor is a compact, prominent assignment status row: “Reserved” in a status lozenge, with “Today · Tue, May 19, 2026”, “Floor P2 · Space D-214”, and the configured “Daily release time · 10:00 AM”. Below it, place one clear single-action button, “Release my space today”, with concise helper text: a release submitted before 10:00 AM becomes claimable at 10:00 AM; one submitted at or after that time is claimable immediately. Do not add a confirmation step. Beside the assignment on wide screens, show Maya Chen’s corporate-directory summary (maya.chen@folio3.com, Product Design, active employee), her registered 2022 Subaru Crosstrek, and the administrator-maintained assignment/floor; stack these sections on narrow screens. Use a real photographic employee portrait of Maya Chen and a real photograph of a 2022 Subaru Crosstrek, never illustrations, substitute icons, or grey image boxes. Show a restrained recent-activity list with “May 18 · Space D-214 · Used” and “May 15 · Space D-214 · Released, not claimed”. Keep the page compact, with intentional whitespace only below the short activity list; all controls must be touch-sized on phone, tablet, and desktop.","S-3":"Design the employee’s Available Spaces page. Use the shared employee navigation in the exact order “My Parking”, “Available Spaces”, “Release Schedule”, “My Vehicles”, with “Available Spaces” active. Lead with a compact availability summary rather than a hero: “3 spaces available now” and “Updated 8:45 AM · Tue, May 19, 2026”. Below, show a tight, readable list of three released allocations with release time, floor, and status: “P2 · released 10:00 AM · Available”, “P1 · released 10:04 AM · Available”, and “P2 · released 10:07 AM · Available”. Each row has a touch-sized “Claim space” action that remains on this page; make clear that the earliest accepted claim wins and that the server assigns the floor from configured capacity. Above the list, show a registered-vehicle selector preselected to Maya Chen’s “2022 Subaru Crosstrek · 7KDL291”; include a real photograph of that vehicle, not an illustration, icon substitute, or grey box. On a successful claim, the page’s confirmation state must name the assigned floor and space (for example, “Confirmed · P2 · T-018”) and state plainly that a confirmed temporary claim cannot be cancelled. Use compact table/list structure on desktop and stacked rows with large touch targets on phones; keep the design system’s restrained SaaS/workforce feel across phone, tablet, and desktop.","S-4":"Design the employee’s Release Schedule page with the shared employee navigation in the exact order “My Parking”, “Available Spaces”, “Release Schedule”, “My Vehicles”; mark “Release Schedule” active. Start with a medium-sized heading and concise explanation: schedule one or more dedicated-space releases up to 30 days ahead. The next section is a compact date picker with multi-date selection, a visible “Select dates” control, and an “Schedule releases” action that stays on this page. Show the configured daily release time, 10:00 AM, beside the date controls. Below it, show a schedule list with believable rows: “Tue, May 26, 2026 · P2 · Scheduled · Claimable at 10:00 AM” and “Tue, Jun 2, 2026 · P2 · Scheduled · Claimable at 10:00 AM”, each with a “Cancel release” action while still unclaimed. Include a claimed example in a subdued recent-history row, “May 18, 2026 · Claimed at 10:12 AM · Cancellation unavailable”, to make the rule clear. On wide screens keep the date controls beside a small rules panel and the schedule list full-width below; stack on phones with touch-sized controls. Do not show photos or decorative artwork. Use the same compact, orderly responsive product styling at phone, tablet, and desktop widths.","S-5":"Design the employee’s My Vehicles page with the shared employee navigation in the exact order “My Parking”, “Available Spaces”, “Release Schedule”, “My Vehicles”; mark “My Vehicles” active. Lead with the heading “My Vehicles” and a short explanation that at least one registered license plate is required for parking release and claim operations. Show Maya Chen’s existing vehicle as a compact row: “2022 Subaru Crosstrek”, plate “7KDL291”, status “Registered”. Include a real photograph of a 2022 Subaru Crosstrek, not an illustration, icon substitute, or grey box. Below the row, provide a clearly labelled registration form for vehicle make/model, license plate, and optional description, with a touch-sized “Add vehicle” action that saves on this page. Use plausible example content in the form hint, such as “2021 Toyota Corolla” and “8ABC123”, without pre-filling a second vehicle. Keep the page focused with no unrelated panels; on wide screens align the form beside a compact vehicle list, and stack them on phones. Maintain a responsive, compact workforce-tool layout for phone, tablet, and desktop.","S-6":"Design the Parking Administrator’s Overview page for Folio3 Parking. Use the shared administrator navigation in the exact order “Overview”, “Employees”, “Parking Settings”, “Access & Reports”, “Utilization”; mark “Overview” active. The first visual element is a compact page heading, “Parking overview”, with an unobtrusive timestamp “Tue, May 19, 2026 · 8:45 AM”. Show a dense but readable set of operational summary figures: “42 dedicated spaces”, “3 released spaces available”, “19 temporary claims today”, and “P2 · 38 configured spaces”. Follow with a short today’s activity table: “8:30 AM · Maya Chen · Space D-214 · Reserved”, “8:41 AM · Arjun Patel · P1 · Claimed”, and “8:44 AM · Priya Nair · P2 · Claimed”. Add a small setup-status section: “Initial administrator setup complete · Leila Haddad · active Folio3 account”. Keep summary figures modest in scale, not oversized cards; place the activity list beside the summaries on desktop and stack sections on narrow screens. Do not show decorative photos or illustrations. Use compact status lozenges, hairline dividers, and touch-sized navigation and controls, responsive at phone, tablet, and desktop widths.","S-7":"Design the administrator’s Employees page with the shared administrator navigation in the exact order “Overview”, “Employees”, “Parking Settings”, “Access & Reports”, “Utilization”; mark “Employees” active. Put a compact heading and employee search/status filters first. Beneath them, use a tight workforce directory table with columns for employee, department, status, parking assignment, floor, and registered vehicles. Include Maya Chen · Product Design · Active · Dedicated D-214 · P2 · Subaru Crosstrek, and Arjun Patel · Engineering · Active · No dedicated assignment · — · Honda Civic. Select Maya Chen and show her profile in a side panel on desktop or below the table on narrower screens: corporate-directory details (name, corporate email, department, active status) are explicitly read-only; administrator-maintained parking assignment, floor, and registered-vehicle records have clear inline create/edit controls. Include Maya’s vehicle “2022 Subaru Crosstrek · 7KDL291” with a real photograph of Maya Chen and a real photograph of the vehicle, never illustrations, substitute icons, or grey boxes. Make it clear that directory details cannot be edited here. Use status lozenges and compact table rows; keep controls touch-sized and adapt the table and profile panel for phone, tablet, and desktop.","S-8":"Design the Parking Administrator’s Parking Settings page with the shared administrator navigation in the exact order “Overview”, “Employees”, “Parking Settings”, “Access & Reports”, “Utilization”; mark “Parking Settings” active. Begin with the heading “Parking settings” and a concise note that changes govern daily release and configured floor capacity. Arrange three clearly separated, compact form sections in user order: “Daily release time” with the current value 10:00 AM; “Floor capacity” with editable rows P1 · 42 spaces, P2 · 38 spaces, and P3 · 26 spaces; and “Work calendar” with Monday–Friday working days, Saturday and Sunday non-working, plus a holiday entry “Memorial Day · May 25, 2026”. Show “Folio3 office time zone · America/Los_Angeles” as a configured setting. Put a single “Save settings” action at the end of the form. On wide screens align release-time and calendar controls beside the floor-capacity table; on phones stack sections in this order. Do not add decorative images. Use compact labeled controls, clear status lozenges where needed, and touch-sized inputs at phone width; maintain the same responsive administrator layout on phone, tablet, and desktop.","S-9":"Design the Parking Administrator’s Access & Reports page with the shared administrator navigation in the exact order “Overview”, “Employees”, “Parking Settings”, “Access & Reports”, “Utilization”; mark “Access & Reports” active. Lead with the heading and a short explanation that role assignment controls access and weekly employee-identifying reports are delivered only to configured recipients who currently have the Parking Administrator role. First show an “Administrator and Security Guard roles” table with named Folio3 employees and editable role controls: Leila Haddad · Parking Administrator, Omar Siddiqui · Security Guard, and Priya Nair · Employee. Include a real photographic portrait for each person, never illustrations, substitute icons, or grey boxes. Below, show the named weekly report-recipient list: Leila Haddad · leila.haddad@folio3.com · Parking Administrator · Included in delivery; Omar Siddiqui · omar.siddiqui@folio3.com · Security Guard · Excluded from employee-identifying delivery. Make the role-based exclusion unmistakable and keep the named list editable; add a “Save access & recipients” action at the end. Use compact HR-style tables and status lozenges, with the role table beside a brief delivery-rule panel on wide screens and stacked sections on phones. Controls must be touch-sized, with the same responsive layout intent for phone, tablet, and desktop."},"theme":{"bodyFont":"IBM_PLEX_SANS","colorMode":"DARK","customColor":"#3FA77A","headlineFont":"HANKEN_GROTESK","roundness":"ROUND_EIGHT"}},"theme":{"basis":[{"ref":"GitHub","took":"Green reserved for the primary action; compact tables and quiet, plain chrome."},{"ref":"Workday","took":"Orderly employee worklists and clear stepwise workforce hierarchy, not its color pairing."},{"ref":"ADP","took":"Sober record treatment and formal, quickly scannable data."}],"brand":"#3FA77A","charts":"soft","chrome":"plain","density":"comfortable","families":{"body":"IBM Plex Sans","heading":"Hanken Grotesk"},"font":"sans","fx":"modern","heading":"match","hero":"none","imagery":"mixed","mark":"glyph","mode":"dark","mood":"Folio3 Parking is one responsive web app","motion":"lively","neutral":"cool","radius":"soft","shell":"auto","surface":"flat"},"themeSource":"new"}